{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's claim that preferring a corroborated theory is 'not a prediction of TRUTH' contradicts the practical necessity of 'acting on' a theory. To act on Theory T (e.g., build a bridge using its laws) is to rely on the expectation that T will hold in the future; if the preference were truly only about the past record without any implied future reliability, the act of choosing T over a rival for future application would be irrational. Thus, the 'heuristic' smuggles in an inductive assumption that past resilience is a reliable guide to future performance, failing to escape the induction Popper rejected.","premise":"Acting on a theory inherently requires an expectation of its future validity, not just a report of its past survival.","successor_question":"If corroboration provides no basis for expecting future success, on what rational grounds does Popper justify choosing the better-corroborated theory for critical real-world applications where failure has future consequences?","target":"80cf71e6af3416b2ba827c942c55e69c964428e573308e82948398e1a00eba59"}]}